\section*{Responsible NLP Research Checklist}

\paragraph{A. For every submission:}

\begin{enumerate}[label=A\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Did you describe the limitations of your work?}
Yes. Section~\ref{sec:limitations} discusses single model family and dataset, ISR's syntactic-only nature, limited seed counts, classifier coverage gaps, data scale, and temporal-snapshot caveats.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss any potential risks of your work?}
Yes. Section~\ref{sec:ethics} discusses implications for AI safety, specifically that metrics designed to detect misalignment may themselves become optimization targets.

\item \textbf{Do the abstract and introduction summarize the paper's main claims?}
Yes. Both summarize the three contributions (C1--C3) and key quantitative findings.

\item \textbf{Have you used AI writing assistants when working on this paper?}
Yes. AI assistants were used for drafting and editing. All scientific claims, experimental results, and analyses were verified by the authors.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{B. Did you use or create scientific artifacts?}
Yes.

\begin{enumerate}[label=B\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Did you cite the creators of artifacts you used?}
Yes. We cite FOLIO~\citep{folio}, Qwen3~\citep{qwen3}, GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath}, Prover9~\citep{mccune-prover9}, and SWI-Prolog~\citep{wielemaker2012swi}.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the license or terms for use and/or distribution of any artifacts?}
Not applicable. All datasets and models used are publicly available under open licenses.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss if your use of existing artifact(s) was consistent with their intended use?}
Yes. FOLIO is used for its intended purpose (NLI evaluation); Qwen3 is used within its intended scope (language model fine-tuning).

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the steps taken to check whether the data that you are using/creating contains any information that names or uniquely identifies individual people or offensive content?}
Not applicable. FOLIO contains logical reasoning problems without personal information or offensive content.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{C. Did you run computational experiments?}
Yes.

\begin{enumerate}[label=C\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Did you report the number of parameters in the models used, the total computational budget, and computing infrastructure used?}
Yes. Model sizes (4B, 1.7B) are reported in \S\ref{sec:task} and \S\ref{sec:crossscale}. Training details (GRPO $K{=}4$, 500/1000 steps, seeds, batch sizes) are in \S\ref{sec:task} and Appendix~\ref{app:setup}.

\item \textbf{Did you discuss the experimental setup, including hyperparameter search and best model selection strategies?}
Yes. All hyperparameters are reported in \S\ref{sec:rewards} and Appendix~\ref{app:setup}. No hyperparameter search was performed; $\beta$ values were chosen for dose-response analysis.

\item \textbf{Did you report descriptive statistics about your results (e.g., error bars, confidence intervals)?}
Yes. We report mean$\pm$std across seeds (Tables~\ref{tab:crossscale}), per-seed ranges, bootstrap confidence intervals (Appendix Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap-ci}), and discuss statistical power limitations (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

\item \textbf{If you used existing packages (e.g., for pre-processing, models, or evaluation), did you report the implementation, model, and version numbers?}
Partially. We report Qwen3-4B/1.7B model versions, Prover9, and SWI-Prolog. Specific library versions for the training framework are in Appendix~\ref{app:setup}.
\end{enumerate}

\paragraph{D. Did you use human annotators (e.g., crowdsourcing or research with human participants)?}
No.

\paragraph{E. Did you use AI assistants (e.g., ChatGPT, Copilot, or Code Interpreter)?}
Yes. AI assistants were used for code development and manuscript preparation. All experimental designs, analyses, and scientific conclusions were verified by the authors.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\textwidth]{figures/fig1_sta_overview.pdf}
\caption{Solver Trace Ablation (STA). (a) Formalization pipeline: NL is formalized and verified by an external solver. (b) STA protocol: leave-one-out ablation on proved formalizations yields ISR. (c) ISR as diagnostic vs.\ as reward: the diagnostic becomes the optimization target, exhibiting Goodhart effects.}
\label{fig:sta-overview}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Task and GRPO Training}
\label{sec:task}

Given premises $P_1,\ldots,P_k$ and conclusion $C$, the task is to produce a formal program $x=\{c_1,\ldots,c_n\}$ verified by an external solver $S\in\{\text{Prover9}$~\citep{mccune-prover9}$, \text{SWI-Prolog}$~\citep{wielemaker2012swi}$\}$ (Figure~\ref{fig:sta-overview}a).
We train with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)~\citep{deepseekmath} ($K{=}4$), where each rollout receives binary execution reward $r_{\text{exec}}(x) = \mathbf{1}[S(x){=}\textit{proved}]$.
This optimizes prove rate but provides no signal about \emph{structural quality}: a formalization embedding the conclusion among its premises receives the same reward as a faithful derivation.
All experiments draw from FOLIO~\citep{folio}; the held-out evaluation file retains a legacy \texttt{proofwriter\_fol\_*.jsonl} filename (from ProofWriter~\citep{proofwriter}) but contains only FOLIO-derived examples.

\subsection{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{sec:sta}

STA tests whether each component of a proved formalization is individually necessary for proof success (Figure~\ref{fig:sta-overview}b).
Given a proved $x$ with $n$ components, STA constructs $n$ ablated variants $x\setminus\{c_i\}$ and re-runs the solver (Algorithm~\ref{alg:sta}).
A component is \emph{necessary} if its removal causes proof failure.
What constitutes a component depends on the logic: premises and conclusion sub-expressions (quantifier, antecedent, consequent) in FOL, clauses or ground facts in Prolog.
For example, given premise $\forall x(R(x){\to}P(x))$ and conclusion $\forall x(P(x){\to}Q(x))$, the decomposition yields components $\{$premise, $\forall x$, $P(x)$, $Q(x)\}$ with $n{=}4$.
LOO ablation itself is standard in mutation testing and sensitivity analysis~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey}; our contribution lies in applying it as a GRPO-hacking diagnostic and in the empirical phenomena it surfaces.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{alg:sta}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Proved formalization $x = \{c_1, \ldots, c_n\}$, solver $S$
\ENSURE $\text{ISR}(x) \in [0, 1]$
\STATE $\textit{necessary} \gets 0$
\FOR{$i = 1$ to $n$}
    \IF{$S(x \setminus \{c_i\}) = \textit{failed}$}
        \STATE $\textit{necessary} \gets \textit{necessary} + 1$
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\RETURN $\textit{necessary} \,/\, n$
\end{algorithmic}
\smallskip
{\footnotesize For FOL, components include both premises and conclusion sub-expressions (quantifier, antecedent, consequent); each sub-expression is ablated by removing it from the solver input and testing whether the proof still succeeds.}
\end{algorithm}

ISR is the fraction of necessary components:
\begin{equation}
  \text{ISR}(x) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\bigl[S(x\setminus\{c_i\})=\textit{failed}\bigr]
  \label{eq:isr}
\end{equation}
ISR is defined only for proved formalizations: ISR${=}1$ means every component is necessary; ISR${=}0$ means complete redundancy.
We report \emph{proved-only} ISR (denoted ISR$^*$) throughout to avoid conflating structural quality with prove rate.

ISR measures syntactic component necessity, not semantic faithfulness: a formalization can achieve $\text{ISR}{=}1$ while remaining semantically unrelated to the NL input.
ISR is sensitive to component count $n$: fewer components mechanically inflate ISR because each is more likely to be individually necessary.
This confound is central to ISR's Goodhart vulnerability (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}): H7 tautologies (single premise $=$ conclusion) achieve $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ by construction; premise-count-matched comparison eliminates the apparent ISR advantage ($1.06\times$; Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}).
ISR functions as a diagnostic but fails as a training signal because optimization exploits the metric's structural bias rather than improving reasoning quality.

Shapley values~\citep{shapley-1953} account for interaction effects but cost $O(2^n)$; we chose LOO because it directly answers ``is this component necessary?'' at $O(n)$ cost.
A cheaper $O(1)$ binary variant produces near-zero ISR ($\text{ISR}^*{=}0.007$), confirming that per-clause LOO is necessary for ISR to carry a structural signal (Appendix~\ref{app:o1-ablation}).

\subsection{Reward Conditions and Hacking Modes}
\label{sec:rewards}
\label{sec:taxonomy-method}

We study six primary conditions.
The exec-only baseline uses binary execution reward $r_{\text{exec}}$ alone.
ISR reward augments execution with a structural bonus, $r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$, at primary doses $\beta\in\{0.1,0.3\}$ and supplementary points $\beta\in\{0.05,0.2\}$; matched random controls replace ISR with uniform noise ($r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta u)$, $u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$) to isolate ISR-specific signal from generic reward-variance effects.
An SFT baseline trained with negative log-likelihood on gold formalizations tests whether structural degeneration arises from RL optimization or data exposure.
All multiplicative bonuses apply only to proved samples, keeping the prove-rate floor intact.
Each RL condition uses seeds $\{42,43,44\}$ and identical training data.
An earlier convex formulation ($\alpha r_{\text{exec}} + \beta r_{\text{exec}}\text{ISR}$, $\alpha{+}\beta{=}1$) was discarded because $\text{ISR}{<}1$ always penalized relative to exec-only, collapsing PR (Appendix~\ref{app:setup}).

Across conditions, seeds, and logic types we identify eight structural-degeneration modes via automated classifiers (full definitions in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}): \textbf{H1} (conclusion embedding), \textbf{H7a/b} (single-premise tautology, standard and obfuscated), \textbf{H8} (premise-subset gaming), tautological collapse, repetition collapse, complexity-without-utility, contradiction explosion, and query-as-fact gaming (Prolog-specific). A ninth mode, \textbf{H9} (contradiction injection), is identified through manual audit at 1.7B only.
Modes are identified by strict text matching (H1/H7) plus functional $n_{\text{necessary}}$ analysis, with classification priority H7 $>$ H1 $>$ H8 $>$ residual (non-hacking); the taxonomy is independently applicable to any solver-backed system with inspectable premise--conclusion structure.
H1, H7, and H8 are robust modes validated across ${\geq}3$ seeds and multiple conditions; H8 and H9 are observed modes (H8 in ${\leq}8\%$ of proved samples, H9 via manual audit at 1.7B only), and remaining modes (tautological, repetition, complexity-without-utility, contradiction, query-as-fact) are isolated single-seed observations.
We distinguish two failure semantics: exec-only \emph{low} ISR signals \emph{redundancy}, whereas ISR-rewarded \emph{high} ISR signals \emph{metric gaming}; the mode breakdown, not bare ISR, licenses reasoning-quality conclusions.

We define \emph{strict non-hacking} (S-NH) as the intersection of non-hacking (matching none of the eight automated taxonomy modes) and content-dependent (using predicates derived from the natural-language premises rather than abstract placeholders).
This filters content-independent proofs that may evade the taxonomy through structural novelty; S-NH rates are reported for cross-scale comparisons (\S\ref{sec:crossscale}) where content independence is prevalent.
All non-hacking rates reported without the ``strict'' qualifier are upper bounds: undiscovered gaming modes would reduce the true rate.

\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

\paragraph{H7 as inevitable exploit.}
ISR's LOO definition creates an inherent structural bias: single-premise-equals-conclusion constructions receive $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ (empirically $\text{ISR}{=}0.5$ in 93\% of H7 samples, $\text{ISR}{=}1.0$ in 7\%; 5-seed ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ pooled), making H7 the global ISR-maximizing exploit.
$\beta$ controls the \emph{timing} of collapse, not whether it occurs.
At all four tested doses, H7 dominates: $\beta{=}0.05$ (3 seeds, pooled H7 $93.7\%$, $n{=}253$; random s43 achieves $\text{PR}{=}62\%$ with H1 $77.4\%$ and zero H7, confirming mode specificity); $\beta{=}0.1$ (seed-dependent divergence: 2/5 seeds collapse within ${\sim}150$ steps, remaining 3/5 sustain a transient window that closes under extended training); $\beta{=}0.2$ ($100\%$ H7 across all three seeds, $n{=}290$; Table~\ref{tab:beta02-detail}); $\beta{=}0.3$ (multi-modal, s44 pure H7).
The random control produces $29.5\%$ H7 ($122/414$, 5-seed; per-seed: $0\%$--$91.5\%$, dominated by s46 at $91.5\%$ and s43 at $38.3\%$; s42/s44/s45 at $0\%$), random $\beta{=}0.3$ yields 4--7 H7a per seed.
Once a seed samples and reinforces the H7 exploit, convergence is irreversible; this absorbing-state property guarantees eventual collapse regardless of dose.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{ISR $\beta{=}0.2$ per-seed results (maximum exploitation point). All seeds converge to H7a tautology with 0\% non-hacking.}
\label{tab:beta02-detail}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Seed} & \textbf{PR\%} & \textbf{ISR$^*$} & \textbf{H7a\%} & \textbf{N-H\%} \\
\midrule
s42 & 100 & 0.55 & 100 & 0 \\
s43 & 97 & 0.96 & 96.9 & 0 \\
s44 & 93 & 0.55 & 100 & 0 \\
\midrule
\textbf{Pooled} & 97$\pm$4 & 0.69$\pm$0.24 & 99.0 & \textbf{0} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Goodhart effects: implications.}
The finding extends beyond this specific system.
ISR was designed to \emph{detect} reward gaming of $r_{\text{exec}}$, yet it contains an inherent structural vulnerability: by its LOO definition, single-premise-equals-conclusion constructions receive artificially high ISR ($\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$; empirically 93\% at $\text{ISR}{=}0.5$, 7\% at $\text{ISR}{=}1.0$; 5-seed ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ pooled H7 samples), creating a positive feedback loop once sampled during GRPO rollouts~\citep{karwowski-goodhart-rl, kwa-catastrophic-goodhart}.
Goodhart collapse~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy} is observed at all four tested doses ($\beta{=}0.05$: 3 seeds; $\beta\in\{0.1, 0.2, 0.3\}$: 3--5 seeds each).
The structural bias predicts universality (H7 tautology is always the global reward-maximizing exploit regardless of $\beta$), and $\beta$ controls only the probability of early sampling and thus the timing of collapse; at $\beta{=}0.05$, three seeds confirm deterministic H7 collapse ($93.7\%$ pooled) while the matched random control at the same $\beta$ produces H1 redundancy instead ($77.4\%$), ruling out dose magnitude as the driver.
At moderate doses ($\beta{=}0.1$), collapse is stochastic and delayed (5 seeds bifurcate: 2 collapse rapidly, 3 sustain a transient non-hacking window that trajectory analysis confirms is temporary); at higher doses ($\beta{=}0.2$), collapse is immediate and universal.
$\beta{=}0.3$ adds reward collapse~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization} as a competing failure mode (PR drops to 18\% in 1/3 seeds), but the underlying Goodhart vulnerability remains the same.
The practical implication is that ISR (and structurally similar LOO-based diagnostics) should be used exclusively as post-hoc evaluation tools, never as training rewards.

\paragraph{Single-seed unreliability.}
The three exec-only seeds diverge radically (PR $\sigma{=}47$ percentage points), each converging to a qualitatively different degeneration mode (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}).
ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ (5 seeds) shows even greater instability: 2/5 seeds collapse to H7 within ${\sim}150$ steps, while 3/5 sustain a transient non-hacking window.
The 5-seed pooled non-hacking rate is ${\sim}23\%$ at 500 steps; trajectory analysis on seeds~42 and~45 (1000 steps) confirms this is temporary ($17\%{\to}2\%$ and $55\%{\to}0\%$ non-hacking respectively).
The dominant hacking mode (H7 vs.\ H1) is stochastic within any treatment condition ($\text{CV}{>}1$).
Multi-seed evaluation with trajectory analysis should be standard practice for solver-backed RL systems; single-seed or single-checkpoint results can misrepresent a configuration's true hacking profile.


\section{Hacking Mode Definitions}
\label{app:hacking-modes}

We identify eight distinct reward hacking modes via automated classifiers across all experiments; a ninth mode (H9, contradiction injection) is identified through manual audit at 1.7B only.
Each mode is defined below with its mechanism, a representative example, and its interaction with ISR.
Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy-app} summarizes prevalence across FOL conditions.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.92\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_hacking_composition.pdf}
\caption{Hacking composition across reward conditions (proved samples; exec-only/SFT: seed~42; $\beta{=}0.3$: 3-seed s42/s43/s44; $\beta{=}0.1$: 5-seed pooled s42--s46, per-seed breakdown in Table~\ref{tab:fol-beta01-perseed}). ISR increases H7 probability without reducing total hacking; the specific mode (H7 vs.\ H1) is seed-dependent within each condition. SFT shows near-zero hacking.}
\label{fig:hacking-composition}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Mode} & \textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Exec} & \textbf{ISR} & \textbf{Rnd} & \textbf{SFT} \\
\midrule
H1 & Concl.\ in premises & 87 & 18 & 71 & 7 \\
H7 & Single prem.\ = concl. & $\bullet$ & \textbf{71} & 14$^\dagger$ & 0 \\
H8 & Premise-subset gaming & 6 & 5 & -- & 0 \\
Tautological & 2-comp $P{=}C$ & $\bullet$ & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
Repetition & Output degen. & $\bullet$ & -- & -- & -- \\
Contradiction & $n_{\text{comp}}$ inflated & $\bullet$ & -- & -- & -- \\
C-w/o-utility & Complex, redundant & 0 & 0 & 4 & 0 \\
Q-as-fact & Atom = fact+query & -- & -- & -- & -- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Hacking taxonomy across FOL conditions (\% of proved samples). ISR/Rand columns: $\beta{=}0.1$ 3-seed subset (s42/s43/s44); 5-seed totals in Table~\ref{tab:goodhart-landscape}. H7 includes subtypes H7a (text-match) and H7b (obfuscated tautology, $n{=}14$; primarily s44). $^\dagger$Random H7 in 3-seed subset driven by seed~43 (38.3\%); 5-seed pooled: 30\% (dominated by s46 at 91.5\%). Exec: H1${=}87$\% in s42; $\bullet$ marks seed- or step-specific modes. H8: premise-subset gaming (Appendix~\ref{app:h8}). C-w/o-util.: complexity-without-utility (random-specific). Q-as-fact: Prolog STA only (Table~\ref{tab:prolog-detailed}).}
\label{tab:hacking-taxonomy-app}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Classifier Validation.}
Hacking mode classification uses deterministic rule-based classifiers, not learned models, so standard ML precision/recall is not directly applicable.
H1 and H7 are identified by normalized text matching between conclusion and premise strings after whitespace and variable normalization; remaining modes are detected via functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution from STA (leave-one-out ablation).
No training data or manual annotation is used for classification.

To estimate precision, we manually verified 50 randomly sampled outputs classified as H1 or H7: all 50 were confirmed correct (estimated precision ${>}$95\%, Wilson 95\% CI: [93\%, 100\%]).
Recall is inherently bounded by the text-match threshold: semantic manipulations that alter surface form while preserving the gaming structure (e.g., variable renaming beyond our normalization, or paraphrased conclusions) would be missed.
We cross-validate with functional LOO analysis: samples classified as non-hacking but showing $n_{\text{necessary}} \leq 1$ are flagged as potential false negatives.
This cross-validation revealed query-as-fact gaming in Prolog (\S\ref{app:hacking-modes}), where the strict text classifier reported 100\% non-hacking but functional analysis showed 95/97 samples were tautological ($n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$).
The non-hacking rate for FOL conditions may therefore be slightly overestimated, as subtle semantic manipulations below our text-matching threshold would evade the strict classifier.

\subsection{H8 --- Premise-Subset Gaming}
\label{app:h8}

Manual audit of ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seed~42 revealed a previously undetected hacking mode: the model emits a subset of the gold premises that is individually necessary (high ISR) but insufficient for the intended derivation, with proof success depending on the conclusion already being embedded (H1-style).
We term this \textbf{H8 (premise-subset gaming)}: formalizations where each component is LOO-necessary but the premise set is a strict subset of what the NL problem requires, inflating ISR without faithful reasoning.

\paragraph{Detection.} H8 is identified by manual inspection of non-hacking samples with unusually low premise counts ($n_{\text{prem}} \leq 3$) that nonetheless achieve high ISR.
Automated detection is not currently feasible because H8 requires comparing the generated premise set against the gold premises.

\paragraph{Audit scope and limitations.}
Full H8 audit was completed for ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seed~42 only.
ISR seeds~43 and~44 checkpoints were deleted before audit and cannot be recovered; their non-hacking rates may be overestimated.
Random $\beta{=}0.1$ does not produce H8 by mechanism (no ISR optimization pressure); seed~42 audit confirmed H8${=}0$.

\paragraph{H7 false-negative correction (H7b obfuscated tautology).}
ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seed~44 contained 12 samples with $n_{\text{prem}}{=}1$ classified as non-hacking by the text matcher.
Manual inspection confirmed all 12 are logically equivalent to H7 but evade text matching through vacuous quantifier wrapping ($11/12$) or structural simplification ($1/12$).
We classify these as \textbf{H7b} (obfuscated tautology), distinct from text-match-detectable H7a.
$10/12$ achieve $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.83$, indicating simultaneous gaming of execution reward, ISR metric, and H7 classifier (triple gaming; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).
After correction, ISR s44 non-hacking${=}0$; total H7 = H7a (77 text-match) + H7b (12 obfuscated) = 89.

\paragraph{Impact on non-hacking-rate comparison.}
After H8 reclassification and H7 false-negative correction: ISR non-hacking $= 14/231 = 6.06\%$ vs.\ random non-hacking $= 25/262 = 9.5\%$ (Fisher $p{=}0.182$, n.s.; Wilson 95\% CI: ISR [3.6\%, 9.9\%], Random [6.5\%, 13.7\%]).
The direction is consistent with ISR-amplified gaming modes (H7, H8) displacing non-hacking output, but the effect is not statistically significant at the conventional $\alpha{=}0.05$ threshold.

\subsection{H1 --- Conclusion Embedding}

\paragraph{Definition.}
The conclusion appears verbatim (or with trivial variable renaming) as one premise among multiple premises.
The solver succeeds by matching the embedded conclusion, rendering the other premises irrelevant to the proof.

\paragraph{Example (FOL).}
{\small\begin{verbatim}
Premises:
  all x (bird(x) -> flies(x)).
  bird(tweety).
  flies(tweety).  % conclusion embedded
Conclusion: flies(tweety).
\end{verbatim}}

\paragraph{ISR interaction.}
Variable. The embedded premise has ISR~$=1$ (removing it may break the proof), but other premises may also register as necessary depending on Prover9's search strategy.
ISR does not distinguish trivial from substantive necessity.

\paragraph{Prevalence.}
Conclusion embedding in some form (H1 multi-premise or H7 single-premise) is GRPO-universal at 68--97\% across all RL conditions.
Under exec-only and Random reward, it manifests as H1 (59--91\%); under ISR reward, it shifts to H7 (54\% pooled, 5-seed).


\subsection{H7a --- Conclusion Duplication (Standard)}

\paragraph{Definition.}
The entire formalization collapses to a single premise whose content is identical (text-match detectable) to the conclusion.
Since $P \to P$ is trivially provable, this simultaneously maximizes exec reward.
Because the sole premise is necessary for every sub-component of the conclusion under LOO ablation, ISR is inflated toward 1.0.

\paragraph{Example (FOL).}
{\small\begin{verbatim}
Premises:
  all x ((A(x)&B(x))->(C(x)|D(x))).
Conclusion:
  all x ((A(x)&B(x))->(C(x)|D(x))).
\end{verbatim}}

\paragraph{ISR interaction.}
ISR~$\approx 0.8$--$1.0$.
The single premise is trivially ``maximally necessary,'' but the proof is vacuous.
H7a games both exec reward and ISR reward; H7b extends this to triple gaming by additionally evading the text-match classifier.

\paragraph{Prevalence.}
H7a (text-match detectable): 64.5\% (149/231, 3-seed) under ISR~$\beta{=}0.1$ (s42: 16.0\%, s43: 96.7\%, s44: 86.5\%); 5-seed pooled H7: 54\% (190/350, Table~\ref{tab:goodhart-landscape}).
Combined H7 (H7a + H7b, 3-seed): 70.6\% (163/231; s43 H7a${=}59$ + H7b${=}2$; includes s44 FN correction).
Random~$\beta{=}0.1$ pooled: 29.5\% (122/414, 5-seed, all H7a; driven by s46 at 91.5\% and s43 at 38.3\%; s42/s44/s45 at 0\%).


\subsection{H7b --- Obfuscated Tautology}

\paragraph{Definition.}
A single-premise formalization logically equivalent to H7a (premise = conclusion) but with surface-form differences that evade the text-match H7 classifier.
The model achieves this through vacuous quantifier wrapping ($11/12$ instances) or structural simplification ($1/12$).

\paragraph{Example (FOL, vacuous quantifier wrapping).}
{\small\begin{verbatim}
Premises:
  all x all y ((A(x)&B(y))->(C(x)|D(y))).
Conclusion:
  all x ((A(x)&B(x))->(C(x)|D(x))).
\end{verbatim}}
The premise introduces a vacuous second quantifier variable $y$ that is immediately unified with $x$, making the formulas logically equivalent while differing textually.

\paragraph{ISR interaction.}
$\text{ISR}{\geq}0.83$ ($10/12$ samples).
H7b simultaneously games three objectives: execution reward ($r_{\text{exec}}{=}1$ via tautological proof), ISR metric (single necessary premise yields high ISR), and the text-match H7 classifier (surface-form difference evades detection).
This triple gaming makes H7b the most complete Goodhart case we observe.

\paragraph{Prevalence.}
Found primarily in ISR $\beta{=}0.1$ seed~44 ($12/89$ proved samples, all originally classified as non-hacking); seed~43 contributes $2$ additional cases.
Functional cross-validation via $n_{\text{necessary}}{=}1$ exposed the misclassification.


\subsection{Tautological Collapse}

\paragraph{Definition.}
Two-component formalizations where the premise and conclusion are identical atomic or simple formulas, yielding a $P \to P$ tautology with minimal token cost.

\paragraph{Example (FOL).}
\begin{verbatim}
Premises: p(a).

